WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
These giant tech companies

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:04.100
have so much power.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.880
Big Tech poses the single greatest threat

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.707
to free speech in our country today.

00:00:08.707 --> 00:00:10.450
So much economic power

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:12.310
and so much political power.

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:13.730
Everywhere you turn these days,

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:15.790
Big Tech companies are under fire.

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:17.984
Instagram's supposedly
addictive and negative effects

00:00:17.984 --> 00:00:20.750
on teenage girls have lawmakers comparing

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:23.900
its parent company,
Facebook, to Big Tobacco.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.880
Conservatives like Florida
Governor Ron DeSantis

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.570
and Texas Governor Greg Abbott

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:30.660
have signed controversial legislation

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.470
banning social media platforms

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:35.180
from moderating content
and kicking off users

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:36.460
for political reasons.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:39.640
Supreme Court Associate
Justice Clarence Thomas

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.050
has said businesses
like Twitter and YouTube

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:44.980
should be subject to
strict federal regulation.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:46.950
Liberal legislators in Colorado

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:50.230
have proposed creating a digital
communications commission

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:51.070
that would have the power

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:53.380
to change how platforms do business

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:56.710
in the name of fighting hate
speech and misinformation.

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:59.870
Lawmakers in at least 38
states have introduced

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.630
over a hundred laws in
the past couple of years

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:05.770
to regulate online speech
and business practices.

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:09.410
In his new book, Tech
Panic, Reason's Robby Soave

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:12.210
says such attacks are
modern-day witch hunts

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:15.580
that fall apart under even mild scrutiny.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:18.720
They're the contemporary
version of past freakouts

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.700
over video games, rock
music and comic books.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.110
We shouldn't fear Facebook
or the future, writes Soave.

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:26.490
The actual threat, he says,

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.470
comes not from private companies,

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:32.000
but from politicians, woke
mobs, social conservatives

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.350
and activists whose real goal

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:36.563
is to limit speech they don't like.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:38.553
Robby Soave.

00:01:38.553 --> 00:01:39.386
Thanks for talking.

00:01:39.386 --> 00:01:40.219
My pleasure.

00:01:40.219 --> 00:01:43.260
Give me the elevator pitch of Tech Panic:

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.410
Why We Shouldn't Fear
Facebook and the Future.

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:47.243
Sure.

00:01:47.243 --> 00:01:50.320
Panic about social media
is very popular right now.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:51.370
It's bipartisan.

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:54.470
It's crossed all over
the ideological spectrum.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:56.920
People as different as
Elizabeth Warren and Josh Hawley

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.300
and Joe Biden and Donald Trump

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:00.720
all think we need to do something,

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.590
the government needs to do
something about social media,

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:04.700
about Big Tech.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:06.657
My book argues no, that's wrong.

00:02:06.657 --> 00:02:09.170
The concerns are overstated.

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:12.273
The solutions that these
people offer are very bad,

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:15.620
from a range of not fixing the problems

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:19.100
that they're talking about
to they'll make things worse

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:20.970
to the problems they're talking
about aren't even problems.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:25.970
So I get into all the various,
the most popular panics

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.220
about social media and discuss

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:29.053
to the extent they're true

00:02:29.053 --> 00:02:29.980
and what should be done about them.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:31.960
Okay, so we'll go through them

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:32.900
and we're gonna come to things

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:34.290
like Section 230

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:38.880
and antitrust actions against Big Tech.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.620
But first, you know, the
kind of panic du jour,

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:47.620
which came up in a big way
after the book was printed,

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.730
has to do with Facebook and Instagram

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:52.450
having a terrible negative effect,

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:53.910
particularly on young girls.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:55.440
This is Instagram.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.320
A report came out, The Wall
Street Journal talked it up

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.530
about Instagram and Facebook researchers

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.130
had looked at the platform itself

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:08.230
and said, oh my God, Instagram
is driving young girls

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:10.270
to anorexia and worse.

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:11.420
What is wrong about that?

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:12.253
Right.

00:03:12.253 --> 00:03:14.960
So it looks to me like Facebook

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:18.096
has come up with some
internal data showing

00:03:18.096 --> 00:03:19.646
a mild

00:03:19.646 --> 00:03:21.550
increase in

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:23.080
feelings of depression

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:24.690
or not being good enough

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:27.860
or not being pretty enough
among teenage girls.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:29.210
I think their figure was a third

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:32.910
of their teenage female users

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:34.630
were experiencing heightened depression

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:35.463
because of Instagram.

00:03:35.463 --> 00:03:38.100
The idea being that Instagram
is showing you more,

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:39.090
in the search feature,

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:41.420
it's curating for you

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:44.450
a feed of images based on things
you've been interested in.

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.410
So you're gonna see more
makeup, more models,

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:47.270
more pretty people.

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:50.380
Also Instagram itself is this
kind of deceptive medium,

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:53.100
where you're seeing the
best, the most glamorous,

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.840
the most attractive version of everyone

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.950
and that can lead to
feelings of insecurity

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:01.320
about your own appearance, et cetera.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:02.720
So I think from looking at

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:06.020
and I looked at a lot of
other data for this book,

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:08.500
if you wanna say, there are reasons to be,

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:09.700
we should be concerned.

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:11.940
You know, we should keep studying this.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:15.680
It might be having a negative
effect on some teenagers.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:16.513
Absolutely.

00:04:16.513 --> 00:04:18.900
I think that could very well be the case

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.250
and we should continue
to look at these things.

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:27.450
The idea that there's some
broad, insurmountable problem

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:31.200
for the vast majority
of social media users

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.430
does not seem well-founded.

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:36.730
You can cherry pick
this data a lot of ways.

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:40.840
For every statistic
about how teenage girls

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:43.140
have become more anxious

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:45.800
or more unhappy in the last 10 years,

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:47.370
you can find one showing that,

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:50.900
well, this other category
of social media user hasn't.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:53.173
It's really hard to measure these things.

00:04:55.293 --> 00:04:57.250
Some of the data looks to me like

00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:02.250
the users who use social media
too much or extreme all day

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:04.270
have some bad mental health outcomes,

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.520
but so do the teens who don't
use social media at all.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:08.440
The ones who are not, who are loners,

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:10.423
who are not connected with their friends.

